WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.340
Many of us have grown reading his tales on
self-destruction, revenge and violence.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.680
Tales of premature burials, of ghastly cats
and ravens, where the line between the living

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.680
and the dead is never clear.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.690
But when you look deeper into the life and
works of Edgar Allan Poe a more complex figure

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:21.970
emerges, beyond the traditional image of the
alcoholic, pale, sickly author with an obsession

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:22.970
for death.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.090
Poe’s life was not easy, in many ways: tragic.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.070
And his path crossed the Grim Reaper’s many
times.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.410
But in today’s Biographics we are going
to look at another angle of Poe.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:37.140
The athletic military man, the rebellious
student, the ruthless reviewer, the father

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.450
of detective fiction.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:43.989
And to satisfy those with morbid fantasies,
we are going to investigate the mystery behind

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:48.309
his death.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:58.890
Thou Art the Man
Edgar Poe was born on the 19th of January

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.860
1809, in Boston, from David and Elizabeth,
two traveling actors.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.289
Edgar’s life had barely started when he
suffered a double trauma.

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:10.890
On the 20th of December 1811 Elizabeth died
of illness, perhaps pneumonia or tuberculosis,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.700
both highly prevalent in the US at that time.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.539
David died within a few days of his wife,
the details are unconfirmed.

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:22.299
According to one account David was performing
in Norfolk, Virginia with his travelling theatre

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:23.862
company, when he also died of an illness.

00:01:23.862 --> 00:01:28.590
A different account tells us that by the time
of Elizabeth’s death, the Poe family was

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.409
a broken one: David had left the household,
leaving his wife destitute.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:33.933
Six days after his mother’s death
[Caption: 26th December 1811]

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:38.380
the orphaned Edgar was fostered into the home
of John and Frances Allan of Richmond.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.009
His sister, Rosalie, was taken in by the Mackenzie
family, also of Richmond, while big brother

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:45.709
Henry remained with the grandparents in Baltimore.

00:01:45.709 --> 00:01:50.149
John and Frances Allan were a childless couple
and although they never legally adopted Edgar,

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:51.799
they considered him to be their son.

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.770
Edgar called them “Pa” and “Ma”.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.799
John was a wealthy merchant, a self-made man
from Scotland.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:00.069
While Edgar’s relationship with Frances
would always be deeply affectionate, him and

00:02:00.069 --> 00:02:02.389
John would frequently clash.

00:02:02.389 --> 00:02:07.109
On the 7th of January 1812 Edgar was baptised
as Edgar Allan Poe.

00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:12.840
The Allan family with Edgar in tow later moved
to London, where they lived from 1815 to 1820.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.349
During these years the young Edgar learned
grammar, Latin, Greek and – surprise – dancing!

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:24.269
The Allans returned to America in 1820 and
by 1823 Edgar was studying in a school run

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.550
by one William Burke.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:33.300
Not the body snatcher and murderer of Burke
and Hare fame … just a name sake.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.969
But still, nice coincidence.

00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:39.500
Like if Stephen King had graduated from Ed
Gein High.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.140
Poe was a very active and athletic teenager,
contradicting the common image of the sickly,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.340
brooding intellectual.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.510
In the summer of 1824 he became famous for
swimming seven miles up the James River, against

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.510
a heavy tide.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.620
That’s almost three times the swimming segment
of an Iron Man competition.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.120
[Caption: Take that, King and Lovecraft!]

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.170
Apparently schoolmaster Burke followed him
in a boat in case he needed help.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.840
Maybe that’s what kept Edgar going – being
chased by a Scottish serial killer on a boat

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:03.970
is a powerful motivator.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.480
But it wasn’t him, just a name sake.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.420
Later that year, Edgar authored his first
poem, of just two lines:

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:16.569
“Last night, with many cares &amp; toils oppres‘d,
Weary, I laid me on a couch to rest”

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:20.880
Which can be paraphrased as
“I am gonna take a nap on the sofa”

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:30.099
Never Bet the Devil your Head
In February 1826 Edgar enrolled in the University

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:31.560
of Virginia, Charlottesville.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.639
But his studies there were short-lived.

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:37.730
The wealthy John Allan had no intention to
fill Edgar’s pockets with easy money, so

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:42.700
the freshman had to subsidise his expenses
with a side hustle: gambling.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.489
Edgar accumulated $2,000 of gambling debts.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:48.239
That’s more than $50,000 in today’s money.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.510
John Allan refused to pay, leading to a rift
between the two men, a situation which would

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:53.670
occur again in the future.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.889
That year, Edgar couldn’t really catch a
break.

00:03:55.889 --> 00:03:59.510
During Christmas holidays he was looking forward
to reuniting with his childhood sweetheart

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:05.829
and prospective fiancée, But Elmira’s parents
had pressured her into dumping him for a wealthy

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:07.579
and solid businessman Alexander Shelton.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:08.579
Yeah, we get it, but could he swim seven miles
chased by a Scottish serial killer?

00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:13.090
It is understandable if in March 1827 Edgar
decided to leave University and reunite with

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.890
older brother Henry in Baltimore.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.220
Still in need of steady income, in May Poe
enlisted in the US Army, under the name Edgar

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.239
A. Perry.

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:23.849
Private ‘Perry’ did well in the Army and
rose to the rank of Sergeant-Major of an artillery

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:25.610
regiment in less than two years.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:26.610
Not only that.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:31.000
He also found time to write his first book
of poetry, ‘Tamerlane and Other Poems’

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.470
published with the pseudonym
“A Bostonian.”

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.710
The booklet sold about 50 copies.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.360
If you come across a copy of ‘Tamerlan’
in your great-great-grandparents’ attic,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:42.070
you could auction it for a nice $172,000.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:46.949
On the 28th of February 1829, Frances Allan,
‘Ma’, died from a respiratory illness,

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:48.729
just like Edgar’s biological mother.

00:04:48.729 --> 00:04:52.819
In the following years Edgar’s behaviour
became erratic and rebellious, probably as

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:55.020
a consequence of Ma’s death.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.590
Initially Poe seemed to like an Army life:
in April of 1829 he applied to West Point

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.580
academy, looking forward to a promotion from
Sergeant to Lieutenant.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:06.449
He entered the academy in the summer of 1830,
but by the following winter he was doing his

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:08.140
best to get kicked out.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:14.290
A once talented cadet, Poe racked up 110 offenses
and 106 demerits in two terms.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:19.650
He was court-martialed and tried at the beginning
of February 1831 for gross neglect of duty.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.340
What was the cause for his rebellion?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.460
Again, it all came from his relationship with
John Allan.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.580
‘Pa’ had remarried, perhaps too quickly,
with Mrs Louisa Patterson, which Poe disliked.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.720
Moreover, Edgar had discovered that John had
sired children out of wedlock.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.729
The feud was embittered by a letter written
by Poe in which he claimed that John Allan

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:37.900
was seldom sober.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.000
Edgar wanted to leave West Point to pursue
writing at this stage, but he needed John’s

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.210
authorisation for a voluntary discharge.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:48.580
When John, now cold and distant, refused,
Edgar started his self-sabotaging campaign.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.379
He succeeded, and was kicked out from the
academy … not before collecting donations

00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:56.270
from 100 cadets to fund his next book of poems,
titled

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.430
“Poems”
In the summer of 1831, fate dealt another

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.900
blow to Edgar, as it claimed the life of his
brother Henry, yet another victim of tuberculosis.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.650
Edgar’s writing career started to pick up
in the early 1830s, and his short story “Message

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:13.110
found in a bottle” won a $50 prize from
a Baltimore magazine in 1833.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.610
The prize was very welcome, John had closed
the coffers.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:21.319
Second wife Louisa did not approve of Edgar,
and plus the couple now had a biological son,

00:06:21.319 --> 00:06:23.370
likely to inherit Allan’s wealth.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.210
In 1834 John fell ill.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:28.110
When Edgar heard the news, he tried to reunite
with him one last time.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.380
They had not seen each other since West Point.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.840
Edgar rushed to Richmond, overcame Louisa’s
resistance and finally came face to face with

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:36.949
the now terminal ‘Pa’.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:39.720
Thomas Ellis, John’s business partner described
the tearful reunion:

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.009
“With what little strength was left in him,
John stood up …”

00:06:42.009 --> 00:06:43.450
Did they hug?

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:46.199
Oh no:
“(John) raised his cane and threatened to

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:48.159
strike him if he came within his reach!”

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:49.940
It was the last time they ever met.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:56.849
John Allan died on March 27, 1834 and his
will - it made no mention of Poe.

00:06:56.849 --> 00:07:03.740
Bridal Ballad
In the second half of the 1830s Poe’s writing

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.220
career took off.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.740
Rather: he was always employed, but he had
little fame and even less money.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.750
Following his Ma’s death in 1829, Edgar
had developed a friendship with his cousin

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.860
Virginia Clemm, 13 years his junior.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:19.159
Virginia was the daughter of Maria, who was
the sister of David Poe.

00:07:19.159 --> 00:07:24.319
So she was a first-degree relative of Edgar’s
via his biological father, not his foster

00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:25.319
family.

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:28.680
This relationship was initially an innocent
one, with Virginia acting as a go-between

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:32.750
for Edgar and a girl called Mary Devereaux,
whom he was trying to woo.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:38.650
By 1835, when Poe was 26 and Virginia only
13, the friendship was developing into something

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:39.650
else.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.979
Despite Virginia’s relatives’ best attempts,
on the 22nd of September 1835, Edgar and Virginia

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:47.310
married in secret, celebrating a public wedding
the following year.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.569
Edgar even took in his aunt-and-mother-in-law
Maria Clemm.

00:07:50.569 --> 00:07:54.389
He nicknamed her ‘Muddy’ for some reason
and apparently loved her as a mother, addressing

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:56.199
her as such in later poem.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:08:00.599
Fun fact: after Poe’s death, Maria was completely
destitute, relying on charitable donations

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:01.599
– among the donors: Charles Dickens!

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:03.039
[Caption: Fun fact: a fun fact involving death
and poverty is not fun.]

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:06.990
Going back to Edgar and Virginia, a marriage
at such a young age would certainly not be

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.250
acceptable by today’s standards, and even
back then it was frowned upon.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:14.159
But some authors claim that Edgar’s and
Virginia’s relationship may have been purely

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:15.159
platonic.

00:08:15.159 --> 00:08:19.430
The theory is that after the death of both
his biological and foster mothers, Poe had

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:23.780
transferred his feelings of filial love to
Aunt Mary, and in turn, to Virginia.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:27.751
You can draw a parallel with Poe’s work
at this stage, namely the short story ‘Morella’

00:08:27.751 --> 00:08:29.889
of April 1835.

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:35.270
In this story, an unnamed narrator regards
“with a feeling of deep yet most singular

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:40.110
affection … my friend Morella”
The two marry and Morella grows obsessed with

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.150
death and issues of identity.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:46.100
She dies in childbirth and the protagonist
decides to leave the baby girl unnamed for

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.100
ten years.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.160
As she grows old, she is uncannily alike her
mother.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.590
When the girl turns ten, the father agrees
to have her baptised.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:58.460
When he chooses the name ‘Morella’, the
girl immediately dies, uttering ‘I am here!’.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.740
The tale concludes:
“with my own hands I bore her to the tomb;

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.680
and I laughed with a long and bitter laugh
as I found no traces of the first in the channel

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.220
where I laid the second.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:10.890
– Morella”
The tale features Poe’s recurring topics

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.450
of resurrection and identity beyond death.

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:17.730
And consider this: the narrator and Morella
are friends, before marrying, just like Edgar

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:18.730
and Virginia.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:23.240
And then here is a transfer of identity from
mother to daughter, which opens the door to

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:27.580
more Freudian nightmares that I can handle
today.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.340
Morella and the husband in the tale had a
happy marriage.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:31.820
How was Edgar’s and Virginia’s?

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.230
Apparently, it was successful, despite lack
of money.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.070
Virginia had a deep adoration towards Poe
and always kept his portrait under her pillow.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:43.770
Edgar, on the other hand, had started flirting
with one Frances Sargent Osgood, but besides

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.650
exchanging letters there is no proof of a
relationship.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:51.130
Frances was being unsuccessfully courted by
another author and journalist, Rufus W. Griswold,

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.060
keep an eye on him.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:57.280
These letters were enough to enrage another
lady, Elizabeth Ellet, who had a crush on

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.280
Poe.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:01.960
Her reaction caused a scandal, as she alleged
Poe and Frances had a physical affair.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.380
She even spread rumours that Poe was
“subject to acts of lunacy.”

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:10.150
Virginia was unconcerned with Poe’s flirtations,
but this scandal affected deeply her frail

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:11.150
health.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:19.550
The Man that Was Used Up
In the second half of the 1830s Poe went full

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:23.740
workaholic, moving frequently across East
Coast cities, looking for work in magazines.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:28.330
He did not neglect his creative writing, though,
publishing his first and only novel, The Narrative

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:31.150
of Arthur Gordon Pym‍ in 1838.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:35.570
In the following two years Poe published two
works: his seminal collection of ground-breaking

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:40.340
short stories ‘Tales of the Grotesque and
Arabesque’; and a treatise on shellfish,

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.710
‘The Conchologist’s First Book’, which
he co-authored.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.040
One of the two sold out in just two months.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.040
Guess which?

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:48.420
[Countdown on screen: 5 … 4 … 3…

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:49.620
2 … 1]
The shellfish.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:50.710
It was the shellfish manual.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:56.020
I am not going to comment on the taste of
American readers in the 1830s, but hey, this

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.990
was Poe’s only commercial success in his
lifetime.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:02.200
But he never made a dime from royalties.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:06.730
In 1841 Poe took a position as editor for
Graham’s Magazine, which he also used as

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:08.580
a platform for his own short stories.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.910
Poe grew the circulation of the magazine from
5,000 to 37,000 subscribers.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:15.830
Poe even convinced Charles Dickens to write
for the Graham!

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:19.730
But in May, Poe left the magazine, handing
over his duties to Rufus W. Griswold.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:20.730
Remember him?

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:24.040
Poe wrote to a friend:
“My reason for resigning was disgust with

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.740
the namby-pamby character of the Magazine
… The salary, moreover, did not pay me for

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:33.100
the labour which I was forced to bestow”
By that time, Virginia had already fallen

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:36.470
ill to tuberculosis, some say as a consequence
of the Ellet scandal.

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:40.940
Her health worsened, but Poe did not stop
on his streak, he was finally going places.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:46.500
In 1843 Poe’s tale ‘The Gold-Bug,” won
a $100 prize from a Philadelphia newspaper.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:50.610
It was followed by a theatrical production
and a French translation, giving Poe some

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.050
deserved public attention.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:56.740
He embarked on a tour of sold-out poetry lectures,
moved to New York, and was a sub-editor of

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:57.740
the Evening Mirror.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:02.320
It was this paper that in January 1845 published
his most famous poem, “The Raven”.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.290
It became an immediate, nation-wide hit.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.740
What princely sum did Poe earn from this masterpiece?

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:09.770
Fifteen dollars.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:12.300
$325 in today’s money.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.430
In February Poe took control and ownership
of the Broadway Journal, but this did not

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.430
help him financially.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:22.450
If anything, he accumulated even more debt,
and the Journal shut down in January next

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:23.450
year.

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:26.610
Things started to pick up thanks to royalties
from a publication about New York’s literary

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:31.860
scene, but life giveth and life taketh away:
Virginia’s condition worsened and by early

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:32.940
1846 she was bedridden.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:36.520
She told a friend:
“I know I shall die soon … but I want

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.950
to be as happy as possible and make Edgar
happy.”

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:45.040
On the 30th of January 1847, Virginia Poe
died of tuberculosis in Fordham, New York.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:49.670
On her deathbed, it is claimed that Virginia
blamed her death on the jealous Mrs. Ellet.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:53.160
Poe’s love for Virginia was consuming, as
was his grief.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.090
For almost two years he visited her grave
every day, keeping it fresh with flowers.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:01.270
What future lay ahead of Edgar Allan Poe now
that her beloved Virginia was gone?

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:06.410
Let’s pause the man’s life for a while,
and let’s dig into his work.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:12.930
Grotesque and Arabesque
Poe is remembered as master of Horror fiction,

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:14.920
and a father of the modern short story.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:19.370
He composed his tales following two main rules:
each story should be short enough to be read

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:23.880
in one sitting; each word should contribute
to the overall effect of the story.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:27.820
His published work came at the apex of the
Gothic and Romantic periods and had a great

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:32.160
influence in Europe, where he was idolised
by Charles Baudelaire and the movement of

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:33.630
the French “Poétes maudits”.

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:37.930
Beyond horror, his body of work encompasses
many other genres and in most cases, it is

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:38.930
not easy to define.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:44.060
Take the short story “The Duc De L’Omelette”:
a French Aristocrat and gourmand dies of disgust

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.370
when a delicacy is improperly served to him.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.700
He wakes up in a Hell and starts plotting
his way out.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:52.160
He challenges Satan to a duel, but alas the
Prince of Hell does not fence.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.020
The next best thing is a nice game of 21,
or Blackjack if you like.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.860
Satan cannot refuse a game of cards: challenge
accepted.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.080
The Duc, a skilled gambler, wins the game,
and his freedom.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:06.910
In four pages Poe defines two characters,
sets up a conflict, establishes some devilish

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:13.070
lore along the way and crosses three genres:
comedy, horror and subtle satire of European

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:14.070
aristocracy.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.410
Another cross-over of styles he achieved with
The Murders of the Rue Morgue.

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:21.940
There is some horror also here, with a killing
perpetrated by a monstrous assailant.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:24.790
But, most of all, the tale is the first Detective
story.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:28.006
Poe established the archetypes of the genre
with the following The Mystery of Marie Roget

00:14:28.006 --> 00:14:29.006
and The Purloined Letter : a brilliant amateur
detective, Auguste Dupin, skilled at observation

00:14:29.006 --> 00:14:30.006
and deduction; a friend who acts as a narrator;
a baffled Police force needing the amateur’s

00:14:30.006 --> 00:14:31.006
help.

00:14:31.006 --> 00:14:32.006
It’s Sherlock Holmes, more than 40 years
earlier.

00:14:32.006 --> 00:14:33.006
The same collection “Tales by EA Poe”,
features The Gold Bug.

00:14:33.006 --> 00:14:35.600
This adventure-slash-detective romp tells
the story of a narrator, his volatile friend

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:41.190
Legrand and a freed slave, Jupiter, who decipher
an encrypted message to find a pirate treasure.

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.790
The story is best remembered for Legrand’s
use of frequency analysis to crack the code

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:49.170
and for the depiction of Jupiter: to modern
ears, it is downright racist.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:53.660
But assigning an important speaking role and
a great deal of action to a black character

00:14:53.660 --> 00:15:00.080
in 1840s America was a rather progressive
move.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:05.100
Bombast
Much of Poe’s writing career was dedicated

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.800
to reviews of other authors.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:10.960
As a literary critic, Poe celebrated the works
of Washington Irving and Nathaniel Hawthorne,

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.100
among the classics of American literature.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.540
But he was known and feared for how he destroyed
less skilled authors.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.000
This earned him the nickname of
The Tomahawk Man

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.410
Tackling the poems of one William W. Lord,
Poe opines that

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:27.060
“the only remarkable things about Mr. Lord’s
compositions are their remarkable conceit,

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.740
ignorance, impudence, platitude, stupidity
and bombast.”

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:34.010
[Caption: Stupidity and Bombast]
“from any farther specimens of your stupidity,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:35.500
good Lord deliver us!”

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:36.500
Ouch.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:37.500
Novelists are no luckier.

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:42.290
Theodore S. Fay’s 1835 work ‘Norman Leslie’
was “unworthy of a school-boy.”

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:46.560
“There is not a single page of Norman Leslie
in which even a school-boy would fail to detect

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:51.880
at least two or three gross errors in Grammar,
and some two or three most egregious sins

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.430
against common-sense.”

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:57.210
The one who gets the full tomahawk treatment
is Morris Mattson’s novel ‘Paul Ulric’:

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:02.300
“The book before us is too purely imbecile
to merit an extended critique — but we shall

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:07.330
have no hesitation … in exposing fully before
the public eye its four hundred and forty-three

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:11.250
pages of utter folly, bombast, and inanity”
[Caption on screen: Bombast and Inanity]

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.570
Poe was not done yet:
“Such are the works which bring daily discredit

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:19.670
upon our national literature … (works) of
incongruous folly, plagiarism, immorality,

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:22.010
inanity, and bombast”
[Caption: Inanity and Bombast]

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:24.960
Poe also attacked fellow journalists, editors
and critics.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.250
The editor of the Boston Miscellany, H.T.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:31.130
Tuckermann, was an
“insufferably tedious and dull writer”

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:33.820
While critic Rufus W. Griswold was dismissed
as a

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:37.790
“toady, destined to sink into oblivion”
Griswold.

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:41.240
We’ll meet him again.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:46.610
The Conqueror Worm
Poe’s reputation still today is one of a

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:50.590
heavy drinker, but actually it was only after
Virginia’s death that he gave in to alcohol.

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:52.930
And even then, he never drank in huge quantities.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:56.320
The problem was that even a small quantity
of liquor could knock him out.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:01.950
In November of 1848 Poe was courting poetess
Sarah Helen Whitman, no relation to Walt Whitman.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:06.390
Mrs. Whitman was so concerned about his reputation
for drinking that asked for total abstinence

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:08.399
from alcohol as a condition for marriage.

00:17:08.399 --> 00:17:11.789
Poe failed to meet the request and Sarah called
off the engagement.

00:17:11.789 --> 00:17:16.110
In the summer of 1849 Poe was on a lecture
tour in Richmond when he met again his once

00:17:16.110 --> 00:17:19.459
girlfriend Elmira, the one who had dumped
him for the businessman.

00:17:19.459 --> 00:17:22.139
Elmira was now a widow and the two rekindled
their love affair.

00:17:22.139 --> 00:17:24.750
In August, Elmira accepted Edgar’s proposal.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:29.250
And Poe must have been dead serious about
it, as only two days later he joined the Sons

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:33.460
of Temperance, a sort of Alcoholic Anonymous
for the 19th century.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:34.789
Everything was working out fine.

00:17:34.789 --> 00:17:38.870
Then, the chain of events started, one that
would lead to Poe’s unresolved death.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:43.639
On the 26th of September 1849, Poe visited
a physician friend called John Carter.

00:17:43.639 --> 00:17:47.639
He was due to leave Richmond, Virginia to
go to Philadelphia to complete an editing

00:17:47.639 --> 00:17:48.639
job.

00:17:48.639 --> 00:17:52.570
Carter advised Poe to postpone his trip by
a few days, but he decided to leave any ways.

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:56.450
On the 27th of September 1849, Poe left Richmond
for Philadelphia.

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:58.010
For the following 6 days, he just vanished.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.759
[Screen fades to black, a caption appears
on screen: Baltimore, Evening of the 3rd of

00:18:01.759 --> 00:18:04.640
October, 1849]
A journalist called Joseph Walker, walking

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:08.809
outside the pub ‘Gunner’s Hall’ came
across a traumatised man, lying in the gutter.

00:18:08.809 --> 00:18:13.639
The man was visibly ill, in a state of confusion
and dressed in worn-out, ill-fitting clothes.

00:18:13.639 --> 00:18:16.910
The man introduced himself as Edgar Allan
Poe and asked for help.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:22.110
Poe asked Walker to contact one Joseph Snodgrass,
a newspaper editor with a medical background.

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:26.030
Snodgrass took Poe to a hospital, where was
treated by Dr John Moran.

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:31.049
Moran noted down his patient’s symptoms:
fever, mania, revulsion to water, calling

00:18:31.049 --> 00:18:32.820
for somebody called
‘Reynolds’

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:37.289
Moran also asked Poe where he had left his
possessions, but the writer could not remember.

00:18:37.289 --> 00:18:41.429
On the 7th of October 1849, Edgar Allan Poe
died, aged 40.

00:18:41.429 --> 00:18:44.740
The reported cause of death was phrenitis,
or swelling of the brain.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:48.529
His mother-in-law, in hearing that Poe had
died, went to check on his cat, Catterina,

00:18:48.529 --> 00:18:50.909
and found out that she had also died.

00:18:50.909 --> 00:18:54.190
One of the early reported causes of Poe’s
death was alcohol abuse.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:58.330
This unverified rumour was spread by Poe’s
obituary, more on this later.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:03.399
Other possible causes include tuberculosis,
epilepsy, diabetes and even rabies – the

00:19:03.399 --> 00:19:06.920
latter may be consistent with Catterina’s
death.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:10.299
Author Matthew Pearl offered another explanation:
brain cancer.

00:19:10.299 --> 00:19:11.919
This is consistent with the symptoms.

00:19:11.919 --> 00:19:17.559
Plus, when Poe’s body was displaced to another
burial in 1875, the coffin broke and witnesses

00:19:17.559 --> 00:19:20.990
noticed a small clump rolling inside Poe’s
skull.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.470
This could have been a calcified tumour.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:27.390
Other theories involve violent crime: Poe
may have been assaulted, beaten up by ruffians,

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:30.610
as claimed in 1867 by biographer E. Oakes
Smith.

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:33.960
But Poe’s body did not display any sign
of external violence.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:35.909
Another crime theory involves ‘cooping’.

00:19:35.909 --> 00:19:38.750
This was a common type of electoral fraud
at the time.

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:42.620
Gangs of thugs would abduct and ‘coop up’
citizens from the streets and force them into

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.800
a disguise so they could vote several times
for the same candidate.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:49.190
The victims were threatened with violence
or rewarded with alcohol.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:53.490
Intriguing theory, mentioned for the first
time in the early 1870s.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.840
Points in favour:
Baltimore at that time was in the middle of

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.019
a local election
Gunner’s hall was a polling station

00:19:59.019 --> 00:20:03.440
Poe was wearing somebody else’s clothes
Baltimore’s newspaper The Republican and

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.929
Argus published warnings against cooping on
the 1st and 3rd of October.

00:20:06.929 --> 00:20:07.929
Now, we are not Dupins, but we have done some
extra sleuthing of our own.

00:20:07.929 --> 00:20:11.210
We searched the enigmatic name ‘Reynolds’
in local Maryland newspapers and we found

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:16.169
that in September there were two people called
Reynold acting as District Delegates in Elkton,

00:20:16.169 --> 00:20:17.590
just outside Baltimore.

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:20.330
Could they be involved in the local Baltimore
elections?

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.700
Was this a name that Poe heard, or read, while
being cooped?

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:27.529
We also spoke with an oncology surgeon, formerly
at one of London’s biggest cancer centres,

00:20:27.529 --> 00:20:29.309
whom we will call “Dr M”.

00:20:29.309 --> 00:20:34.559
Dr M confirmed that tumoural masses can calcify
even when the surrounding healthy tissue decomposes.

00:20:34.559 --> 00:20:39.970
He offered an additional explanation for the
clump rolling inside Poe’s skull: a head

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:40.970
trauma.

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:44.039
Not only it can cause death, but it can also
cause a small bone between the cheekbone and

00:20:44.039 --> 00:20:48.669
the jaw to fracture and detach, free to rattle
inside the skull.

00:20:48.669 --> 00:20:51.960
A blunt trauma at the back of the head would
not leave visible injuries – and Poe’s

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:54.580
body did not display any sign of violence.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:57.409
There are many more theories, so I am going
to stop there.

00:20:57.409 --> 00:20:59.899
Gun to the head, I would go with cooping plus
the blunt trauma.

00:20:59.899 --> 00:21:05.169
Why don’t you tell us your theory in the
comments?

00:21:05.169 --> 00:21:10.880
Nevermore
On the 9th of October 1849 an obituary of

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:14.210
Poe’s appeared on the New York Tribune,
signed “Ludwig”.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:20.100
We don’t know if Poe’s death was an assassination,
but certainly this article was character assassination.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:24.559
While praising Poe’s work, ‘Ludwig’
wrote that few will be grieved by his death,

00:21:24.559 --> 00:21:29.919
that the writer had little to no friends and
led a dissolute and dissipate life.

00:21:29.919 --> 00:21:34.540
Ludwig would publish yet more articles praising
Poe the author while destroying Poe the man,

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:37.379
exaggerating or inventing embarrassing details.

00:21:37.379 --> 00:21:40.550
These articles were now signed with his real
name:

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:42.360
Rufus W. Griswold.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:46.429
The irony is that Griswold was Poe’s executor,
meaning he had the rights to edit and publish

00:21:46.429 --> 00:21:52.110
his works for a nice profit, all the while
cementing the image of the dissolute and lunatic

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.149
alcoholic still surviving today.

00:21:54.149 --> 00:21:59.320
I hope you have learned something new about
Edgar Allan Poe today, please spread the word.

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:05.700
We had to skip tons of stuff but if you want
to dig deeper check out the website of the

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:12.059
Edgar Allan Poe Society of Baltimore, www.eapoe.org,
it’s a real mine of free material, including

00:22:12.059 --> 00:22:13.440
his full works.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:18.499
And if we hear somebody repeating one of Griswold’s
lies … well, like Poe’s Raven, let’s

00:22:18.499 --> 00:22:20.200
just say
“Nevermore!”

